"# Chapter 8 — Sound and Music

The last chapter in this book leaves the screen and goes to the speakers. Sound is a first-class citizen in Motoi, and it is a citizen made of numbers. A note is a *frequency*. A sample is a *number you compute*. A melody is a *list*. A beat is a *schedule*. The round-trip Alfred keeps naming applies to audio the same way it applies to pixels: what the composer saves is what a text editor sees is what the runtime plays — and underneath it all is arithmetic you can read.

Four steps: compute one sample, build a note from a frequency, turn a melody into data, and drive a drum machine with the real synth family Motoi ships.

## Why sound is just math

A sound is a pressure wave. A pure tone is a sine wave, and a sine wave is a function you already have: `math/sin`. Feed it an angle and it returns a number between -1 and 1.

```
(math/sin 1.0)
;; => 0.8414709848078965
```

That single number is the *amplitude* of the wave at one instant. String thousands of them together, sixty thousand a second, and you have sound. Everything else in this chapter — pitch, melody, rhythm — is a way of choosing *which* numbers to string together.

You do not have to leave the language to reason about music. The frequency is a number, the sample is a number, the whole waveform is a list of numbers. It is Scheme all the way down.

## Problem 1 — Compute a sample of a tone

A tone has a frequency in Hertz (cycles per second). To get the amplitude at time `t` seconds, feed `math/sin` the angle `2 * pi * freq * t`.

```
(define pi 3.141592653589793)

(define (sample freq t)
  (math/sin (* 2 pi freq t)))

(sample 440 0)
;; => 0
```

At `t = 0` every sine wave is at zero — the start of the cycle. A tiny step later it has risen:

```
(sample 440 0.001)
;; => 0.36812455268467775
```

`440` is the frequency of the tuning A above middle C — the note every orchestra tunes to. The `sample` function does not care which note it is; it just evaluates the wave at an instant. Feed it a stream of times and you get the shape of the tone.

## Problem 2 — Build a note from a frequency, and the whole scale from arithmetic

Musicians name notes with letters; the machine wants frequencies. The bridge is one formula: each semitone multiplies the frequency by the twelfth root of two. Starting from A4 = 440, a note `n` semitones away is `440 * 2^(n/12)`.

```
(define (pitch semis)
  (* 440 (expt 2 (/ semis 12))))

(pitch 0)
;; => 440       ;; A4, the reference
```

Twelve semitones is an octave — exactly double the frequency:

```
(pitch 12)
;; => 880       ;; A5, one octave up
```

Now the whole scale falls out of a list of semitone offsets. A major scale is the pattern `0 2 4 5 7 9 11 12`:

```
(define major (list 0 2 4 5 7 9 11 12))

(map pitch major)
;; => (440 493.8833012561241 554.3652619537442 587.3295358348151
;;     659.2551138257398 739.9888454232688 830.6093951598903 880)
```

Every pitch in that list is real, computed, and yours to change. Reverse `major` for a descending scale. Add `1` to every offset to transpose up a semitone. The scale is data, and arithmetic is the instrument.

## Problem 3 — A melody is a list of (freq dur)

A melody is two things woven together: pitches and durations. Model each note as a `(freq dur)` pair; the whole melody is a list of them.

```
(define (note semis dur) (list (pitch semis) dur))

(define tune
  (list (note 0 0.5)     ;; A,  half a second
        (note 2 0.5)     ;; B
        (note 4 1.0)     ;; C#, a whole second
        (note 0 0.5)))   ;; A again

;; the pitches
(map car tune)
;; => (440 493.8833012561241 554.3652619537442 440)

;; the total running time, folded up
(fold + 0 (map cadr tune))
;; => 2.5
```

Read the shape. `note` turns a semitone offset and a duration into a `(freq dur)` pair. `tune` is a list of them. `map car` pulls out the pitches; `map cadr` pulls out the durations; `fold + 0` adds the durations into a total length.

Every technique here reduces music to two ingredients: a list of pitches and a rule for placing them in time. Change the list, change the tune. Interleave two lists for a two-voice pattern. The melody is data you can slice, reverse, transpose, and measure — all with the list toolbox from Chapter 2.

## Problem 4 — Turn a waveform into a buffer of samples

To actually hear a note you render it: pick a sample rate, then compute one sample per tick across the note's duration. `iota` gives you the tick indices; `map` turns each into a sample.

```
(define pi 3.141592653589793)
(define (sample freq t) (math/sin (* 2 pi freq t)))

;; render `freq` for `n` samples at sample-rate `sr` (samples per second)
(define (render freq n sr)
  (map (lambda (i) (sample freq (/ i sr))) (iota n)))

;; a toy render: 4 samples of a 1 Hz wave at 8 samples/sec
(render 1 4 8)
;; => (0 0.7071067811865475 1 0.7071067811865476)
```

Read it: `iota n` produces `(0 1 2 ... n-1)`, the tick numbers. For each tick `i`, the time is `i / sr` seconds, and `sample` evaluates the wave there. The result is a buffer — a list of amplitudes an audio backend can push to the speaker.

At `sr = 8` and `freq = 1`, the four samples trace the first quarter of a sine wave: `0`, up to `0.707`, up to the peak `1`, and back toward `0.707`. That is the waveform, made of numbers, one `map` away from silence.

## Capstone — A drum machine with the real synth family

Motoi ships a wired drum-and-orchestra family: `synth/808` (a TR-808 style kit), `synth/orch` (an orchestral blend), and `synth/sp1200` (a sampler). Each pushes a shape-preserving event onto the audio timeline and returns `'ok` — so kids can compose a beat even on a machine with no sound card, and the events still exist for a real backend to render.

```
(synth/808 'kick)
;; => ok

(synth/808 'snare)
;; => ok
```

The 808 voices are symbols: `'kick`, `'snare`, `'closed-hat`, `'open-hat`, `'clap`, `'cowbell`, `'rimshot`, and the toms. Build a bar as *data* — a list of `(beat voice)` events — then play it by walking the list.

```
(define bar
  (list (list 0 'kick)
        (list 1 'snare)
        (list 2 'kick)
        (list 3 'snare)))

(define (play-bar events)
  (map (lambda (evt) (synth/808 (cadr evt))) events))

(play-bar bar)
;; => (ok ok ok ok)   ;; four hits scheduled
```

`bar` is a beat pattern written as plain data — kick on 1 and 3, snare on 2 and 4. `play-bar` walks it and fires each voice with `synth/808`. Every hit returns `'ok`, so the whole bar returns a list of `ok`s: proof that four events landed on the timeline.

The orchestral and sampler voices work the same way — call and they schedule:

```
(synth/orch 'strings 'brass)
;; => ok
```

Change the `bar` list and you change the beat. Add a `(list 0 'closed-hat)` and you add a hi-hat. Concatenate two bars for a longer loop. The pattern is data; the synth family turns data into scheduled sound. Nothing is hidden in a binary project file; the beat is Scheme.

